# Details of conversions and rounding

5.8 FP -> Int
When NaN or Inf cannot be represented in dest fmt: if no other means for indicaing it, Invalid Exc raised
When number converts to Int outside dest range: just like above

When result value differs from source value, Inexact is raised depending on op family:

Don't signal:
― intFormatOf-convertToIntegerTiesToEven(source)
etc

Do signals Inexact:
― intFormatOf-convertToIntegerExactTiesToEven(source)
etc


5.9 Round to integral
rounding without "Exact" doesn't signal exceptions other than SNaN input

― sourceFormat roundToIntegralExact(source) signals Inexact if result not equal to input 


5.11 Details of comparisons
Four mutually exclusive relations are possible: less than, equal, greater than, and unordered; unordered
arises when at least one operand is a NaN. Every NaN shall compare unordered with everything, including
itself. Comparisons shall ignore the sign of zero (so +0 = −0). Infinite operands of the same sign shall
compare equal.

The only possible Exc is Invalid.
	ops with "Signalling" raise it when NaN input (Q or S)
	ops with "Quiet" raise only when SNaN input



# 6. Infinity, NaNs, and sign bit
6.1 Infinity arithmetic
  no exceptions generated, including:
	― add(inf, x), add(x, inf), sub(inf, x), sub(x, inf), for finite x
	― mul(inf, x), mul(x, inf), for finite or infinite x != 0
	― div(inf, x), div(x, inf), for finite x
	― squareRoot(+inf)
	― rem(x, inf) for finite normal x
	― conversion inf into inf in another format
  exceptions generated only when
	― inf is an invalid operand (see 7.2)
	― inf is created from finite operands by Ov (see 7.4) or Div0 (see 7.3)
	― rem(subnormal, inf) signals Underflow

6.2 Operations with NaNs
	SNaN:
		uninitialized variables and arith-like enhancements (such as complex-affine inf or extremely wide range) that are not in the scope of this standard. 
	QNaN:
		should afford retrospective diagnostic information inherited from invalid or unavailable data and results.


	If Invalid excpetion, FP result is QNaN

	SNaN: shall be reserved operands, signal invalid operation exception (see 7.2) for every GenComp and SigComp op, (except conversions described in 5.12. - not appl)


	Every GenComp and QuietComp op with input QNaNs, shall signal no exception, except FMA might signal the Invalid (see 7.2).
	For op QNaN inputs, except as stated otherwise, if FP result is to be delivered, the result shall be a canonical QNaN

	FMT conversions, might be unable to deliver the same NaN. 
	QNaN signals exc on some ops that do not have FP result: comparison and conversion to a format that has no NaNs, (5.6, 5.8, and 7.2)

6.2.1 NaN encodings in binary interchange formats
	Exponent: all 1s
	Mantissa: QNaN: 1st bit is 1
						SNaN: 1st bit is 0, some other must be 1


6.2.3 NaN propagation
	If any NaN input, result is NaN with the same payload as one of input NaNs (not specfied which) 
	NaN payload must be unaffected by conversion to wider format and back


6.3 The sign bit
	X + -X = +0 if rounding is not o Negative
	X + -X = -0 if rounfing towards NEgative
	z + z and z -(-z) where z is zero: always preserve sign of z

	Addition in FMA follows the same rules
	sqrt(-0) = -0  (no exception!)


# 7. Exceptions

7.2 Invalid operation

	For ops with FP result: default result when op that signals InvOp is qNaN that should provide some diagnostic info (see 6.2).
	a) any general-computational op on a signaling NaN (see 6.2), except for some conversions (see 5.12)
	b) multiplication: multiplication(0, inf) or multiplication(inf, 0)
	c) fusedMultiplyAdd: fusedMultiplyAdd(0, inf, c) or fusedMultiplyAdd(inf, 0, c) unless c is qNaN;
		if c is qNaN t-> implementation defined whether we signal InvOp
	d) add, sub, FMA: magnitude subtraction of infinities, add(+inf, −inf)
	e) div(0, 0) or div(inf, inf)
	f) rem(x, y), when y is zero OR x is infinite -  AND neither is a NaN
	g) squareRoot if the operand is less than zero (BUT sqrt(-0) == -0)?
	//h) quantize .....

	For ops without FP result:
	i) any signaling-computational op on a sNaN (see 6.2); then, under default exception
		handling, the operation is evaluated with qNaN in place of the sNaN operands to
		determine the result, which for unordered-signaling comparisons might signal another iInvOp
	j) conversion of FP number to int format
			when the source is NaN OR inf, 
			OR output value falls outside range of result format under the applicable rounding
	k) comparison by way of unordered-signaling predicates listed in Table 5.2, when the operands are unordered
	l) logB(NaN), logB(inf), or logB(0), when logBFormat is int format (see 5.3.3).

7.3 Division by zero
	The Div0 - iff an exact inf result is defined for op on finite operands. default result is inf, correctly signed according to op:
	― div: when the divisor is zero and the dividend is a finite non-zero number -> sign is XOR of the operands’ signs (see 6.3).
	― For logB(0) when logBFormat is FP format, the sign is minus.

7.4 Overflow  !! tricky details TODO
	overflow iff destination format’s LargestFiniteNumber is
	exceeded in magnitude by what would have been the rounded floating-point result (see Clause 4) were the
	exponent range unbounded. The default result shall be determined by the rounding-direction attribute and
	the sign of Intermediate as follows:
	a) roundTiesToEven AND roundTiesToAway: all Ov to inf with sign of Intermediate.
	b) roundTowardZero:  all Ov to LargestFiniteNumber with sign of the Intermediate
	c) roundTowardNegative: positive Ov to LargestFiniteNumber, negative Ov to −inf
	d) roundTowardPositive: negative Ov to MostNegativeFiniteNumber, positive Ov to +inf.
	In addition, under default exc handling for Ov, Ov flag shall be raised and Inexact shall be signalled

7.5 Underflow  !! tricky details TODO
	The underflow exception shall be signaled when a tiny non-zero result is detected. For binary formats, this
	shall be either (implementation CHOICE)
	a) after rounding — when a non-zero result computed as though the exponent range were unbounded
	would lie strictly between ± b emin, or
	b) before rounding — when a non-zero result computed as though both the exponent range and the
	precision were unbounded would lie strictly between ± b emin

	In addition, under default exception handling for underflow, if the rounded result is inexact — that is, it
	differs from what would have been computed were both exponent range and precision unbounded — the
	underflow flag shall be raised and the inexact (see 7.6) exception shall be signaled. I

	If the rounded result is exact, no flag is raised and no inexact exception is signaled. This is the only case in this standard of an
	exception signal receiving default handling that does not raise the corresponding flag. Such an underflow
	signal has no observable effect under default handling.


7.6 Inexact
	Except as specified otherwise (e.g., 5.8 and 5.9), op delivering numerical result that signals no
	other exc shall signal Inexact if its rounded result differs from hypothetical result of (both exponent range and precision unbounded).
	The rounded result shall be delivered to the destination

	the inexact flag is always
	raised when either the overflow or underflow flag is raised.


Exception types:
	Invalid
	Div0
	Overflow
	Underflow
	Inexact



# CPU state needed

32 x 64b registers

Control: 

FP on/off - 1 bit

4 rounding modes - 2 bits
- RM_Even 	roundTiesToEven (default)
- RM_Up 	roundTowardPositive
- RM_Down 	roundTowardNegative
- RM_Zero 	roundTowardZero

5 exceptions - 10 bits (5 trap enable, 5 flags)
	Invalid
	Div0
	Overflow
	Underflow
	Inexact
